Under federal law (FTC), insider trading has two main elements: first, you must know something confidential is a material fact; second, you must take action to report it before you trade or convey that information. Most jurisdictions require a written report that identifies the material facts, the person trading, the amount of material facts, and the timing of the trade. Most brokers and exchanges also require a signed disclosure form before processing any trade to verify you're not selling or trading on information protected by law.

Common defenses include:
- You made the trade in good faith;
- You didn't know it was material;
- You weren't at fault;
- The trade was too small to be affected or material.

If caught, penalties range from fines up to $500,000 and imprisonment in some cases. The FTC has specific guidelines on filing for civil penalties, though enforcement actions are rarely taken against people discovered rather than individuals.

A few important notes: this is complex area. A qualified financial advisor can review your specific situation and help you decide whether to report anything and if so, how to structure the disclosure to minimize risk.
